<h1>brute force string search</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
Find a <a href="string.html"><em>string</em></a> in another string or body of text by trying each position one at a time.  There are many far faster <a href="stringMatching.html"><em>string matching</em></a> algorithms.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Also known as</strong> naive string search.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="stringMatching.html"><em>string matching</em></a>, <a href="bruteforce.html"><em>brute force</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="strsrch.html"><em>strsrch</em></a>.
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
Christian Charras' and Thierry Lecroq's <a href="http://www-igm.univ-mlv.fr/~lecroq/string/" target="_blank">Exact String Matching Algorithms (C)</a>, <a href="https://users.dcc.uchile.cl/~rbaeza/handbook/algs/7/711a.srch.c" target="_blank">(C and Pascal)</a>

<hr>

Go to the
<a href="https://www.nist.gov/dads/">Dictionary of Algorithms and Data
Structures</a> home page.

<hr>

